ocsp — provenance note
======================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `bip340`/
`dtls`/`falcon` crypto modules). The closest sibling, `x509`, is likewise a
clean-room RFC implementation.

Provenance
----------

ocsp — clean-room implementation from RFC 6960 ("X.509 Internet Public Key
Infrastructure Online Certificate Status Protocol - OCSP"), covering the
OCSPRequest / OCSPResponse / BasicOCSPResponse / ResponseData / SingleResponse /
CertID ASN.1 structures (Appendix B), the response-verification model
(§4.2.2.2 authorized responders, id-kp-OCSPSigning EKU, CertID binding,
producedAt/thisUpdate/nextUpdate freshness) and the id-pkix-ocsp-nonce
extension. RFC 6960 is a public IETF specification — not a copyrightable work
(merger doctrine; see CONVENTIONS.md §5) — so no third-party-source NOTICE
entry is required for the spec citation alone. No reference implementation's
source (OpenSSL, BoringSSL, or any other) was ported or consulted.

Built on the public APIs of sibling modules and Zig std only:

- `x509` (this repo) — `extensions.parseElement` (bounds-safe DER element
  reader) and the extended-key-usage walk
  (`findExtensions`/`iterate`/`parseExtKeyUsage` + `Purpose`); the
  `id-kp-OCSPSigning` decision itself is local (stricter than `hasPurpose`,
  which also accepts `anyExtendedKeyUsage`).
- `rsa` (this repo) — RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5 verification (RFC 8017 §8.2).
- `p256` (this repo) — ECDSA-P256 verification.
- `std.crypto.hash.Sha1` / `sha2.Sha256`/`Sha384`/`Sha512` and
  `std.crypto.Certificate.der` types (MIT, part of Zig itself).

Test fixtures in `ocsp_test.zig` are constructed (signed with the sibling
`rsa`/`p256` modules), not captured from any third party. `goldens.zig` (added
2026-08-01) additionally holds two REAL OCSP responses captured live from
public CA responders (DigiCert, GoDaddy) plus the certificate chains they
answer for — see SPEC.md "Fixture provenance" for what each exercises.

Attribution for the captured responses: NONE required, by the same reasoning
CONVENTIONS.md §5 and root NOTICE §0 give for running an installed
third-party binary as a black-box compatibility test oracle (e.g. `tar`/`nft`
goldens elsewhere in this repo) — no third-party source or design was
consulted, only the observable output of a live server implementing the
public RFC 6960 wire protocol was captured, exactly as those local-binary
captures are. A signed OCSP response's bytes are themselves almost entirely
protocol-structure (ASN.1 DER dictated by the RFC) plus facts (timestamps, a
serial number, a cryptographic signature) — not copyrightable expression —
so this is neither a vendored third-party corpus (root NOTICE §1, e.g.
`csvstream`'s CSV acid-test suite) nor a design reference (§2): no entry in
either place, and no root NOTICE change accompanies this addition. See
`goldens.zig`'s own doc comment for the exact capture recipe and what each
response exercises that the constructed fixtures do not.

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.
